For years, Volkswagen has repeatedly ranked as one of the most reliable car brands on the market. However, even the most reliable brands have their fair share of issues, and the 2022 Volkswagen Tao is no exception.

If you own an affected 2022 Volkswagen Tao, you should be able to get the issues repaired free of charge. Yet sometimes, a recalled vehicle may be beyond repair.

In that case, you may be able to seek compensation through [California lemon law](/info/how-it-works). The lemon law is a legal protection that helps California consumers get refunds or replacement vehicles when their cars are defective.

In order to qualify as a lemon in California, a vehicle must:

- Be covered under the manufacturer or dealer warranty when the defect or nonconformity was first reported; and

- Have one or more defects/nonconformities that substantially impair the vehicle’s use, value, or safety; and

- Have been subjected to a reasonable number of repair attempts by a manufacturer-certified facility to fix the defect or nonconformity; or

- Have been out of service for repairs on one or more defects/nonconformities for at least 30 days; and

- The defect or nonconformity must not have been caused by driver abuse or neglect.

If your Volkswagen meets these criteria, you may be entitled to seek compensation for your vehicle.

So, what do you need to know about 2022 Volkswagen Taos recalls - and what steps can you take if your vehicle is affected? Let’s discuss this.

## Engine Control Unit (ECU) VW Taos Recall

One defect that has been affecting the 2022 Volkswagen Taos is the Engine Control Unit (ECU). In some vehicles, the ECU software can cause unintended deceleration and stall-like behavior. This is a serious safety risk, as it could leave you vulnerable to being hit by another vehicle if your car suddenly stalls on the highway or a busy road.

This Volkswagen recall has affected over 5,000 Taos. If your car is included, make sure to have the problem repaired as soon as possible to protect your safety and that of others on the road.

This issue should only require a simple software update to resolve. However, in some cases, the defect may indicate a deeper problem with your car. Suppose your car has been experiencing recurring issues with the ECU - and the manufacturer is unable to repair it with a reasonable number of attempts. In that case, it’s important to seek legal advice to determine whether you may have a California lemon law case on your hands.

## Suspension Knuckle Volkswagen Taos Recall

Another 2022 Volkswagen Taos recall was issued for certain models equipped with all-wheel drive. The left or right rear suspension knuckles in affected vehicles might corrode, crack, or separate. This could lead to tire damage, loss of vehicle control, and increase your risk of a crash.

So far, roughly [2,500 VW Taos](https://www.cnet.com/roadshow/news/volkswagen-taos-tiguan-recall-rear-suspension-knuckle-crack-break/) have been recalled due to this issue, and owners are advised to have the faulty suspension knuckle(s) replaced as soon as possible. Volkswagen should rectify the problem at no cost to you.

Since this defect is a clear safety risk, your car may qualify as a lemon if the problem persists after bringing it in to get repaired. However, you’ll need to give Volkswagen a reasonable amount of [repair attempts](https://www.consumer-action.org/downloads/english/CA_lemon_law_2015_EN.pdf) before filing a California lemon law claim.

## Rearview Camera 2022 Volkswagen Taos Recall

[Certain 2022 VW Taos](https://www.usatoday.com/recalls/automotive/NHTSA-22V514000/) equipped with the optional rearview camera system are also subject to a Volkswagen recall. This issue stems from a software problem that could lead to the camera not working properly. In turn, this could block your view of the road, increase the risk of a crash, and leave you vulnerable to other safety risks.

If your vehicle is included in this 2022 Volkswagen Recall, VW should resolve the problem at no additional cost. You should have your car checked out as soon as possible so the vehicle operates seamlessly.

Again, don’t settle for a temporary fix. Instead, contact a lemon law attorney in California if you’re experiencing repeated issues with your rearview camera. These attorneys understand the nuances of a California lemon law case and can help you fight for the compensation you deserve.

## Fuel System Volkswagen Recall

The last Volkswagen Tao recalls you should know about involves the fuel system. In certain vehicles, the under-hood fuel supply connector may detach from the engine. If this happens, you may be exposed to dangerous fuel leaks and fires, which could result in serious injury or death.

Volkswagen has already recalled [over 16,500 Taos](https://www.consumerreports.org/car-recalls-defects/volkswagen-recalling-taos-suvs-for-a-potential-fuel-leak-a9123692672/) due to this issue. If your car is included in the Volkswagen recall, take it to your nearest dealership as soon as possible so that they can resolve the problem at no cost to you.

Although Volkswagen has narrowed down the potential causes of this issue, they still haven’t been able to pinpoint exactly what the root of the problem is. If you’re having issues with your fuel system that persist after multiple repair attempts, it’s worth seeking legal advice to determine whether you may have a California lemon law case on your hands.

